oor  get-off-the-pot situation right because  either you're actually going to do this  and you're going to commit  to long-term to this player that you  have now tagged twice  or you're just saying you know what this  is the last year that we're going to be  able to do this because as you just said  mike  based on the way the franchise tag works  you can't really tag this guy again  you're not going to ever pay a safety  quarterback money and you really  shouldn't because that is one of those  positions  where you can find somebody else who can  at least do an adequate job  if not you know a pro bowl or second  team all-pro job  like justin simmons and i feel like i  should point out no relation  was able to do last year for the denver  broncos so  he's a very good young player i mean we  just showed the graphic of where he was  on our top 100 list of free agents and  i think the broncos would do well to  sign him to a long-term deal  however if they can't if simmons feels  like you know what i'm gonna probably be  able to make more money on the open  market next year  then i can understand why he wouldn't  necessarily want to be in this spot in  denver  where contention is not necessarily all  that guaranteed  the balance for him is a little more  delicate than it would be for a dak  prescott or a quarterback because  the injury risk the wear and tear  despite what happened last year to dak  prescott it was a fluke thing for the  most part quarterbacks are less likely  to become physically beaten down over  the course of a year  justin simmons would have to carry that  injury risk and the risk that all of a  sudden he's not as good as he was after  a year  and he gets the 13.729 million  plus whatever he gets next year assuming  that risk that maybe he won't be as  attractive next year but  that's the challenge for the broncos now  that the tag has been applied and we're  just four days away from the closing of  the window and presumably the broncos  just decided w